# Copy this file to .env and fill in your own keys.
# .env is gitignored and will never be committed.

# ── LLM Provider ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Auto-detected from whichever API key is present (DeepSeek → Anthropic → OpenAI).
# Set LLM_PROVIDER explicitly to override:
#   LLM_PROVIDER=deepseek   (default model: deepseek-chat)
#   LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic  (default model: claude-sonnet-5)
#   LLM_PROVIDER=openai     (default model: gpt-4o)
# LLM_PROVIDER=deepseek

# ── Option A: DeepSeek ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Sign up at https://platform.deepseek.com/api-keys
DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=sk-your-deepseek-key-here
# DEEPSEEK_MODEL=deepseek-chat         # optional; default: deepseek-chat
# DEEPSEEK_API_BASE=https://api.deepseek.com  # optional; change for local proxy

# ── Option B: Anthropic Claude ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Sign up at https://console.anthropic.com/
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-your-key-here
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5      # optional; default: claude-sonnet-5

# ── Option C: OpenAI ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Sign up at https://platform.openai.com/api-keys
# OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-your-openai-key-here
# OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4o                  # optional; default: gpt-4o
# OPENAI_API_BASE=https://...          # optional; set for Azure or local proxy

# ── Web search (required for patent & market search) ───────────────────────────
# Set one of these. If both are set, TAVILY_API_KEY wins.
#
# Serper proxies Google search and, like similar scraping-based APIs, may
# reject requests from cloud/datacenter IP ranges — confirmed blocking every
# request (plain and site:-restricted) from two Railway regions, while the
# same key worked fine from a home network. Tavily is built for server-side
# callers and was not observed to have this problem; prefer it for any cloud
# deployment. Both are optional to configure — only one is required.
#
# Sign up at https://serper.dev/api-key  (free tier: 2 500 queries/month)
SERPER_API_KEY=your-serper-key-here
# Sign up at https://tavily.com  (free tier: 1 000 credits/month, no card required)
# TAVILY_API_KEY=your-tavily-key-here

# ── Optional: Semantic Scholar API Key ────────────────────────────────────────
# Raises rate limit from 1 req/s to 10 req/s. System works without it.
# Apply at https://www.semanticscholar.org/product/api
# SEMANTIC_SCHOLAR_API_KEY=your-s2-key-here

# ── Optional: Lens.org Patent API Key (free after registration) ───────────────
# Enables structured patent search via Lens.org (>120M global patents).
# Without this key the system falls back to Serper for patent search.
# Sign up (free) at https://www.lens.org/lens/user/subscriptions
# LENS_API_KEY=your-lens-key-here

# ── Optional: NCBI API Key (free) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Raises PubMed E-utilities rate limit from 3 to 10 req/s.
# Apply at https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/account/
# NCBI_API_KEY=your-ncbi-key-here

# ── Optional: API rate limit ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Default 6 req/min suits DeepSeek free tier. Raise for OpenAI/Anthropic.
# MAX_RPM=20

# ── Optional: OpenTelemetry/OpenInference tracing ─────────────────────────────
# Disabled by default. When enabled, the worker emits redacted CrewAI, model
# provider SDK, retrieval, and evaluation spans to Phoenix (or another OTLP-compatible
# collector). This never makes extra LLM/search calls, and collector failure
# is recorded as degraded observability rather than failing the paid report.
#
# Local Phoenix:
#   docker run --rm -p 6006:6006 arizephoenix/phoenix
#   AGENT_OBSERVABILITY_ENABLED=true
#   PHOENIX_COLLECTOR_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:6006/v1/traces
#
# Phoenix Cloud uses the endpoint and API key shown by your Phoenix project.
# Inputs, outputs, prompts, tool definitions, embeddings, and the capability-
# bearing run id are excluded by code even if environment defaults change.
# AGENT_OBSERVABILITY_ENABLED=true
# PHOENIX_COLLECTOR_ENDPOINT=https://app.phoenix.arize.com/v1/traces
# PHOENIX_API_KEY=your-phoenix-key-here
# PHOENIX_PROJECT_NAME=academic-commercialization-agent
# AGENT_OBSERVABILITY_FLUSH_TIMEOUT_MS=2000

# ── Legacy variable names (still accepted as fallbacks) ───────────────────────
# OPENAI_API_KEY / OPENAI_API_BASE / OPENAI_MODEL_NAME all map to DeepSeek
# when LLM_PROVIDER=deepseek, for backward compatibility with older setups.

# ── Optional: public deployment access gate ────────────────────────────────────
# Set one of these before exposing the API on the public internet (e.g. a
# cloud demo link). Every /api/ request must then carry a valid code in the
# X-Access-Code header — the frontend prompts for it once and remembers it.
# Leave both unset for local use; that disables the gate entirely, so nothing
# here is required to run the project locally.
#
# ACCESS_CODE is one code for everyone. ACCESS_CODES is a comma-separated
# list — hand a distinct one to each person, and each code's run history
# (the sidebar) only ever shows runs made under that same code, never
# anyone else's. All three of these may be set at once; any one alone is
# enough to turn the gate on.
# ACCESS_CODE=choose-a-long-random-string
# ACCESS_CODES=code-for-alice,code-for-bob,code-for-carol
#
# ACCESS_CODE_ADMIN authorizes runs like any other code (own owner tag, own
# daily-cap budget) but is exempt from the per-code filter on GET /api/runs
# — its holder sees every code's history combined, to check who has
# actually used their code without checking each one individually.
# ACCESS_CODE_ADMIN=choose-another-random-string

# ── Optional: daily run cap ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Hard ceiling on total runs per UTC day, independent of concurrency — a
# second line of defence if a code ever leaks. Applied separately to each
# access code (including the admin one), not as one pool every code shares.
# 0 (default) disables it.
#
# Size this against your search quota, not your LLM bill. Measured over 30
# runs: a run issues a median of 9 web searches (range 5-15), which is well
# under the ~22 queries it constructs — collection stops early once a domain
# has enough sources, and academic retrieval goes to OpenAlex/PubMed/arXiv
# rather than the search API. Tavily's 1000/month free tier is therefore
# roughly 110 runs in total across every code.
# API_DAILY_RUN_CAP=3

# ── Optional: request rate limit ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Requests per minute per client, refilled continuously. Distinct from the two
# caps above, which count *runs*: this counts *requests*, and exists because
# the endpoints reachable without a code (a run addressed by its id, and the
# gate check itself) cost nothing to serve and are otherwise unbounded.
# Charged per access code where one is present, per address otherwise, so
# several people behind one campus NAT are not throttled as a single client.
# Default 300; 0 disables it. The web client polls progress every 1.2-4s and
# capacity once a second, so two open tabs already sit near 100/min.
# API_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=300

# How many of the concurrent slots visitors bringing their own key may hold at
# once. Defaults to one below API_MAX_CONCURRENT, i.e. one slot that only an
# access-code holder can take.
#
# BYOK runs are exempt from the daily cap, because the visitor pays for their
# own tokens — which is exactly what made them able to occupy every slot, and
# a submission whose key is wrong still holds one while it fails. Without this
# the people you handed a code to could be shut out by anonymous traffic that
# costs you nothing.
# API_BYOK_MAX_CONCURRENT=2

# ── Optional: run retention ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Days a finished run is kept before it is deleted automatically. 0 (default)
# keeps runs forever, which is right locally where outputs/ is your own.
#
# A public deployment is different. Reading a run needs only its id — the
# deliberate report-sharing model — so a link stays live as long as the run
# does, and a visitor who uploads an unpublished paper leaves its contents,
# the contribution extracted from it, and the resulting assessment on your
# server indefinitely. This is the bound on that. /health reports the value
# and the web client shows it, so nobody is surprised by a deletion.
# RUN_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cost reporting
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every run reports its token count per agent. Turning those tokens into
# dollars needs a price, and prices change — so the built-in table is stamped
# with the date it was believed correct, and a model that is not in it reports
# tokens with no dollar figure rather than a $0.00 that reads as "free".
#
# Set this to price a model the table does not know, or to correct one it
# does. USD per 1M tokens, "input:output" or "input:output:cached". Applies to
# every model, which is enough because a run uses one. The cache-read rate
# defaults to the full input rate rather than to zero: assuming cache reads
# are free understates the bill, and understating is the direction nobody
# audits.
# LLM_PRICE_PER_MTOK=0.27:1.10:0.07
